Nondeterministic search can also be used in combination with other search techniques. For example, by applying a nondeterministic search when a maximum is found in hill climbing, the problems of local maxima (the foothill problem) can be avoided.

## 5.16 Island-Driven Search

Island-driven search assumes that an island exists roughly half way between the root node and a goal node. The method involves finding a path between the root node and the island, and a path between the island and a goal node. If no path exists that goes through the island, the method reverts to another search method that ignores the island.

This method is useful in situations where it is extremely likely that the island actually does lie on a path to the goal, for example, if we are trying to identify a route between Milan and Naples, given the knowledge that all roads lead to Rome.

